The North Carolina Workers’ Compensation Act provides a safety net for injured workers. It is a no-fault system, meaning that you do not have to prove that your employer was at fault for your injury. Instead, the focus is on whether your injury occurred in the course and scope of your employment. This is a critical distinction. If your injury happened while you were doing something that was a normal part of your job duties, then it is considered “in the course and scope” of your employment. If it happened while you were doing something outside of your job duties, then it may not be covered. For example, if you are a cashier at a grocery store and you get hurt while bagging groceries in the parking lot, that might not be covered because it was not part of your job duties. However, if you were hurt while scanning items at the checkout lane, that would likely be covered because it was a normal part of your job. The North Carolina Workers’ Compensation Act also provides for certain benefits. These include:

•    Medical treatment for your injury or illness;
•    Temporary total disability (TTD) benefits, which provide a portion of your wages while you are unable to work;
•    Permanent partial disability (PPD)
